{
  "description" : "GCRA rate limiting (rate-limiting contract sections 1, 2, 3a), decided by the memory driver with a pinned clock. policy = {limit, periodNanos, burst}; T = periodNanos / limit (integer division) and the window is burst * T. scenarios: a fresh limiter with the default policy and maxKeys; each step is (at, key, cost[, policy]) in nanoseconds on one monotonic clock (only differences matter). For cost c: tat = min(max(stored, at), at + window); next = tat + c * T; allowed when at >= next - window, then next is stored; else limited with retryAfter = next - window - at and tat stored only when the stored value was beyond the cap. expect: result allowed | limited | error (IllegalArgumentException: cost outside 1..burst of the policy used), remaining ( allowed only: (at - (next - window)) / T), resetAfterNanos (allowed: next - at; limited: tat - at) and retryAfterNanos (limited only). A key is stored per name; when the table holds maxKeys keys and the key is new, buckets with stored <= at are purged (at most once per second of clock) and a key still not fitting shares one overflow bucket. policyChecks: whether a policy can be built (limit and burst 1..1000000, period 1ms..1 day, period/limit >= 1000 ns, burst * T <= 2^63-1 over 4). names: whether a limiter name is accepted ([a-z][a-z0-9_-]{0,62}). The redis driver works in microseconds, truncating T to whole microseconds; the vector's numbers are whole microseconds so both agree.",
